WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.110 --> 00:00:01.943
- [Interviewer] All right, Sylvia.

00:00:01.943 --> 00:00:04.180
Sylvia, where'd you grow
up? Where are you from?

00:00:04.180 --> 00:00:06.690
- I actually grew up in
Lake Forest, California

00:00:06.690 --> 00:00:08.008
in South Orange County. (chuckles)

00:00:08.008 --> 00:00:09.102
- [Interviewer] Okay, you're
an Orange County girl.

00:00:09.102 --> 00:00:10.390
- [Sylvia] (chuckling)
Yeah, born and raised.

00:00:10.390 --> 00:00:13.387
- [Interviewer] Okay, and
what got you down to Skid Row?

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:17.660
- I actually came to LA to go to rehab.

00:00:17.660 --> 00:00:20.630
And I was in rehab there in East LA,

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:23.370
and then I was sober for three years,

00:00:23.370 --> 00:00:25.120
and then I relapsed when
my grandfather died.

00:00:25.120 --> 00:00:26.880
And I had met somebody out here,

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.700
and his mother is actually
out here on Skid Row.

00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.010
And when I relapsed, we
came and visited her,

00:00:32.010 --> 00:00:33.720
and we kinda just got stuck down here.

00:00:33.720 --> 00:00:35.790
So, that was about three years ago.

00:00:35.790 --> 00:00:37.870
- [Interviewer] What was your
drug, or what is your drug?

00:00:37.870 --> 00:00:41.930
- Heroin and meth, and now
I'm on fentanyl too, so.

00:00:41.930 --> 00:00:43.600
- [Interviewer] Oh wow.

00:00:43.600 --> 00:00:46.040
How'd you get introduced
to drugs like that

00:00:46.040 --> 00:00:47.650
down in Orange County?

00:00:47.650 --> 00:00:51.790
- I started using drugs at
like 13, and I partied a lot,

00:00:51.790 --> 00:00:55.280
and was, it just progressed
into the next drug, next drug,

00:00:55.280 --> 00:00:58.360
and eventually, I was sitting
in my best friend's house

00:00:58.360 --> 00:01:01.290
with her mom, who, she was smoking heroin,

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.760
and I was already on
meth, and I seen her mom,

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:07.690
and I asked her mom for a hit,
and her mom gave me a hit.

00:01:07.690 --> 00:01:10.870
And from there on, I just
became out of control

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:14.495
with my heroin habit, yeah. (chuckles)

00:01:14.495 --> 00:01:15.590
- [Interviewer] Now, tell
me about your childhood.

00:01:15.590 --> 00:01:17.330
You grew up with both parents, one parent?

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:20.290
- No, actually, I was
adopted at the age of six.

00:01:20.290 --> 00:01:23.370
At two years old, I was
taken from my mom's custody.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:26.380
She had been with a guy
who was an alcoholic

00:01:26.380 --> 00:01:27.870
and addicted to the crystal,

00:01:27.870 --> 00:01:30.700
and he would physically
abuse me really bad,

00:01:30.700 --> 00:01:35.700
like step on me, almost like,
almost borderline torture.

00:01:36.220 --> 00:01:39.700
When I was taken, the day I
was taken from her custody,

00:01:39.700 --> 00:01:42.740
he had stepped on my head,
and he had an imprint,

00:01:42.740 --> 00:01:46.250
imprinted not just on my
skin, but on my skull,

00:01:46.250 --> 00:01:48.600
the bottom of his Timberland boot.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.220
And so, I was taken from my mom's custody,

00:01:51.220 --> 00:01:53.420
and it was a long custody battle.

00:01:53.420 --> 00:01:55.270
I was in group homes, foster homes.

00:01:55.270 --> 00:01:59.180
Eventually, my mom's aunt and
uncle were able to take me in.

00:01:59.180 --> 00:02:01.950
And it was like a fairytale-like family.

00:02:01.950 --> 00:02:06.390
It was four kids, two boys,
two girls, in Orange County,

00:02:06.390 --> 00:02:09.250
with a beautiful home,
and had the green grass,

00:02:09.250 --> 00:02:11.640
and the pets and everything,
the mom, the dad.

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:12.970
And it was like any child

00:02:12.970 --> 00:02:15.860
would want to be adopted into that family.

00:02:15.860 --> 00:02:17.970
But a month after I was adopted,

00:02:17.970 --> 00:02:21.950
my so-called father,
adopted father, had left.

00:02:21.950 --> 00:02:25.140
He left my mom, five kids.
She was a stay-at-home mom.

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:26.090
He just up and left,

00:02:26.090 --> 00:02:29.080
and she had to go from
being a stay-at-home mom

00:02:29.080 --> 00:02:31.387
to providing for all of us, you know?

00:02:31.387 --> 00:02:34.270
And I love my mom for that
because she did it, you know?

00:02:34.270 --> 00:02:35.470
- [Interviewer] But
it's not your real mom.

00:02:35.470 --> 00:02:36.303
- No, that's my adopted mom.

00:02:36.303 --> 00:02:37.845
- [Interviewer] Adopted mom.
- Yeah.

00:02:37.845 --> 00:02:40.910
She didn't let that stop her

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:43.120
from raising her kids, you know?

00:02:43.120 --> 00:02:45.700
She went to work, she provided for us.

00:02:45.700 --> 00:02:47.920
We were able to continue to stay

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:52.070
in that Orange County lifestyle, you know?

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:53.740
I can't say that we were spoiled,

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.363
but we had everything we needed.

00:02:56.240 --> 00:02:58.930
And now that I'm older
and I look back, man,

00:02:58.930 --> 00:03:00.040
we lived on a lake,

00:03:00.040 --> 00:03:02.360
and my mom was able to do that, you know?

00:03:02.360 --> 00:03:05.750
It wasn't easy for her,
but she was able to do it.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:08.200
And I respect her so much
now for that, you know?

00:03:10.160 --> 00:03:14.440
But unfortunately, for whatever
reason, I'm the only one,

00:03:14.440 --> 00:03:18.986
out of all kids, to end up
on drugs like I am, you know?

00:03:18.986 --> 00:03:20.572
(chuckles)

00:03:20.572 --> 00:03:25.230
- [Interviewer] When did you
get first introduced to it?

00:03:25.230 --> 00:03:28.380
- [Sylvia] I don't know, I
feel like it was always around,

00:03:28.380 --> 00:03:30.740
just with the group of
kids that I was around.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:32.030
I wasn't hanging out
with the popular kids.

00:03:32.030 --> 00:03:34.200
I was hanging out with
the stoners, you know?

00:03:34.200 --> 00:03:37.970
It just, the culture of
drugs was always around me,

00:03:37.970 --> 00:03:39.653
and I glamorized it.

00:03:41.420 --> 00:03:46.420
Heroin addiction, in my life,
in my mind, was glamorized.

00:03:46.450 --> 00:03:49.733
It was like a beautiful tragedy.

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:55.500
Almost every girl I knew wanted
to be that dope-fiend girl,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.333
for whatever reason,

00:03:56.333 --> 00:03:58.900
'cause she always got saved, or, you know?

00:03:58.900 --> 00:04:02.323
So, it was always glamorized
in my head, in my life.

00:04:03.370 --> 00:04:05.324
But it's not so glamorous,
(chuckling) you know?

00:04:05.324 --> 00:04:07.583
It's hard, you know?

00:04:08.960 --> 00:04:10.360
- [Interviewer] So where
are you living now?

00:04:10.360 --> 00:04:11.193
- [Sylvia] In Skid Row.

00:04:11.193 --> 00:04:13.565
- You're on the street?
- (chuckles) In tents, yeah.

00:04:13.565 --> 00:04:15.399
- [Interviewer] And what
do you do for money?

00:04:15.399 --> 00:04:17.390
What a lot of the girls are doing?

00:04:17.390 --> 00:04:20.180
- No, actually, I don't do that,

00:04:20.180 --> 00:04:24.713
and I don't know, I think
it's God or whatever.

00:04:26.796 --> 00:04:30.950
I will hustle, I will sell
things, I will sell, I sell dope.

00:04:30.950 --> 00:04:33.561
Yeah, I chose to,

00:04:33.561 --> 00:04:36.570
'cause, as messed up as it sounds,

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.300
I'm good at selling dope. (chuckles)

00:04:38.300 --> 00:04:41.053
I sell heroin, I sell
crystal, I sell fentanyl.

00:04:42.510 --> 00:04:46.430
But it's hard to be a female out here

00:04:46.430 --> 00:04:48.200
'cause none of these dope dealers,

00:04:48.200 --> 00:04:51.370
they're all gang members, men,

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:55.593
they don't want to see
a female up-do them.

00:04:57.000 --> 00:04:59.360
'Cause any person out here
would rather buy drugs

00:04:59.360 --> 00:05:02.520
from a pretty-looking girl
than a hardcore gang member.

00:05:02.520 --> 00:05:06.713
And so, I get,

00:05:09.460 --> 00:05:14.460
it's hard for me to make any
money out here selling dope

00:05:14.480 --> 00:05:17.233
because they don't want to
see me make it, you know?

00:05:19.600 --> 00:05:20.860
The crazy thing is, out here,

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:24.180
I came out here with my
boyfriend at the time,

00:05:24.180 --> 00:05:26.670
and me and him ended up breaking up.

00:05:26.670 --> 00:05:28.990
He turned gay on me. (chuckles)

00:05:28.990 --> 00:05:32.750
And I met somebody else.

00:05:32.750 --> 00:05:36.510
And I never thought I would fall in love

00:05:36.510 --> 00:05:37.810
with somebody on Skid Row,

00:05:38.900 --> 00:05:41.860
but he's the only person in my life

00:05:41.860 --> 00:05:44.190
that's ever tried to get
me off drugs, you know?

00:05:44.190 --> 00:05:46.950
And even my family left
me out here for dead.

00:05:46.950 --> 00:05:48.060
I hate my family.

00:05:48.060 --> 00:05:49.970
I don't want nothing to ever do with them,

00:05:49.970 --> 00:05:52.760
because I never hurt them,
I never robbed from them,

00:05:52.760 --> 00:05:56.460
I never stole from them,
but they left me out here.

00:05:56.460 --> 00:05:58.270
And when I asked them for help,

00:05:58.270 --> 00:05:59.650
no, I didn't ask them for money,

00:05:59.650 --> 00:06:00.766
I didn't ask them for anything,

00:06:00.766 --> 00:06:05.400
I asked them for a program,
to get me out of here,

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:07.340
they denied me of that.

00:06:07.340 --> 00:06:11.380
But this man, who was a
hardcore gang member, a killer,

00:06:11.380 --> 00:06:14.550
who has no heart, is the only man

00:06:14.550 --> 00:06:17.293
that's ever offered to
help me get out of this.

00:06:18.440 --> 00:06:19.940
And a lot of people

00:06:19.940 --> 00:06:23.680
have the wrong conception

00:06:23.680 --> 00:06:26.420
of people out here.

00:06:26.420 --> 00:06:28.820
When I first came out
here, I was so judgmental.

00:06:29.710 --> 00:06:31.853
I was like, "Fuck living in a tent.

00:06:33.450 --> 00:06:34.770
I'm gonna sleep in my car."

00:06:34.770 --> 00:06:37.370
When I lost my car, I
had to stay in a tent.

00:06:37.370 --> 00:06:41.980
But now, my family that I
choose to have, my family

00:06:41.980 --> 00:06:44.203
are these people out here that I met.

00:06:45.140 --> 00:06:48.420
My sister, not my sister in my family,

00:06:48.420 --> 00:06:50.300
'cause I don't, those
aren't my family no more.

00:06:50.300 --> 00:06:51.450
But my sister out here,

00:06:52.420 --> 00:06:54.010
she's the only one
that's ever encouraged me

00:06:54.010 --> 00:06:56.790
to keep going and keep
doing better, you know?

00:06:56.790 --> 00:07:01.270
And it's weird because
my whole outlook on life

00:07:01.270 --> 00:07:03.470
has changed, being out here.

00:07:03.470 --> 00:07:04.920
I don't want to die out here.

00:07:05.950 --> 00:07:07.870
I don't want to be another girl

00:07:07.870 --> 00:07:10.690
found in a tent, burned alive, you know?

00:07:10.690 --> 00:07:15.300
I've had one of my closest
homeboys die in a tent,

00:07:15.300 --> 00:07:18.333
go into the coroner's
office as a John Doe.

00:07:19.530 --> 00:07:23.110
And I don't want that to be me. (sniffles)

00:07:23.110 --> 00:07:26.913
And my family, they
don't know where I'm at.

00:07:28.300 --> 00:07:29.780
They don't know if I'm alive,

00:07:29.780 --> 00:07:33.570
and I don't care because
they don't care to find out.

00:07:33.570 --> 00:07:35.370
I have a Facebook, they can find me.

00:07:36.490 --> 00:07:40.960
But these people that I'm now
choosing to make my family,

00:07:40.960 --> 00:07:42.610
they check on me every day.

00:07:42.610 --> 00:07:43.540
They make sure I'm okay.

00:07:43.540 --> 00:07:46.723
They make sure I'm fed, clothed, healthy.

00:07:47.560 --> 00:07:50.140
My boyfriend now, he,

00:07:50.140 --> 00:07:53.320
'cause I had an eating
disorder, I wouldn't eat,

00:07:53.320 --> 00:07:55.763
he makes sure I eat every day.

00:07:56.730 --> 00:07:58.020
And it's little things like that

00:07:58.020 --> 00:08:00.930
that people don't know matter
to somebody, (sniffles)

00:08:00.930 --> 00:08:03.280
but they matter, you know?

00:08:03.280 --> 00:08:07.740
And I've had my heart changed
for people out here (sniffles)

00:08:07.740 --> 00:08:10.430
because I'm one of these people out here.

00:08:10.430 --> 00:08:13.083
I never thought I'd be
somebody to live on Skid Row.

00:08:14.470 --> 00:08:17.803
But I can say that I stay on Skid Row.

00:08:18.830 --> 00:08:23.120
But it gave me a heart,
a heart that I never had,

00:08:23.120 --> 00:08:27.113
a heart to care for somebody
that nobody wants to care for.

00:08:28.170 --> 00:08:31.600
My ex-boyfriend's mom has been out here

00:08:31.600 --> 00:08:33.990
since she was 15 years old.

00:08:33.990 --> 00:08:36.823
She has HIV and lung
cancer, and she's dying.

00:08:37.850 --> 00:08:41.140
Nobody checks on her. Nobody feeds her.

00:08:41.140 --> 00:08:42.763
All they do is feed her crack.

00:08:44.250 --> 00:08:47.193
One day I'm gonna find her
dead in her tent. I know I am.

00:08:48.730 --> 00:08:51.420
And what is anybody gonna do about it?

00:08:51.420 --> 00:08:53.750
People are dying out here every day,

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:57.343
and it's like, what is
anybody doing about it?

00:08:59.690 --> 00:09:04.003
I'm just one girl stuck up
here. I try to make it home.

00:09:05.600 --> 00:09:07.170
I don't know what my next step is,

00:09:07.170 --> 00:09:10.040
but I don't, I'm not
gonna be out here forever.

00:09:10.040 --> 00:09:15.040
I'm 25 years old, and I don't
want to stay stuck out here.

00:09:15.980 --> 00:09:20.720
Some of my closest friends are
those people that talk about

00:09:20.720 --> 00:09:24.530
how they were gang members and murderers.

00:09:24.530 --> 00:09:27.920
And my best friend,
she's been a prostitute

00:09:27.920 --> 00:09:30.320
since she was 14 years old.

00:09:30.320 --> 00:09:34.535
But I love her because she's
real, and she has a heart.

00:09:34.535 --> 00:09:38.330
(sniffles) I always thought
I was better than everybody

00:09:38.330 --> 00:09:39.610
because I was from Orange County,

00:09:39.610 --> 00:09:43.973
because I grew up in a good
lifestyle, a good home,

00:09:45.170 --> 00:09:46.813
but I'm not better than nobody.

00:09:50.893 --> 00:09:55.013
I'm just as low as everybody else.

00:09:55.890 --> 00:09:56.823
And I'm okay with-

00:09:56.823 --> 00:09:57.656
- [Interviewer] You're just
as free as everybody else.

00:09:57.656 --> 00:09:58.590
- I'm okay with that,

00:09:58.590 --> 00:10:01.610
because when you're at your lowest point

00:10:01.610 --> 00:10:03.610
is where you get your greatest strength.

00:10:04.840 --> 00:10:08.350
And my next move in my life,
people are gonna be shocked

00:10:08.350 --> 00:10:11.713
because I've set in my
heart my next steps.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:14.920
Three things to keep secret

00:10:14.920 --> 00:10:19.280
are your finances,

00:10:19.280 --> 00:10:21.293
your love life, and your next move.

00:10:22.820 --> 00:10:25.743
And my next move is gonna
be my greatest move.

00:10:27.570 --> 00:10:30.947
And my life isn't gonna
be nothing what it is now,

00:10:30.947 --> 00:10:33.010
and I'm gonna make sure of it,

00:10:33.010 --> 00:10:36.660
because nobody else can
but me, you know? (sobs)

00:10:36.660 --> 00:10:39.870
And I miss my family every day,

00:10:39.870 --> 00:10:42.090
and it hurts me, but fuck them,

00:10:42.090 --> 00:10:44.993
because they didn't care
if I was alive or not.

00:10:45.920 --> 00:10:48.690
Every single one of my
siblings have gotten married,

00:10:48.690 --> 00:10:51.050
and I'm never gonna be in
one of those wedding books.

00:10:51.050 --> 00:10:52.560
And I'm okay with that,

00:10:52.560 --> 00:10:54.703
because they're not
gonna be in mine either.

00:10:56.689 --> 00:11:01.689
And it's kind of like a harsh
thing to hear, or for to say,

00:11:01.770 --> 00:11:03.780
but it's really how I feel, you know?

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:08.193
Because I never wronged them,
I never did them any wrong,

00:11:09.420 --> 00:11:13.940
but they treated me like I
did, and that hurt. (sniffles)

00:11:13.940 --> 00:11:18.050
And it's just, every day I wake up,

00:11:18.050 --> 00:11:19.633
and I see where I'm at,

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:23.563
and it's a harsh reality of
what I chose to do with my life.

00:11:24.720 --> 00:11:26.530
I haven't done anything.

00:11:26.530 --> 00:11:29.030
I have not graduated high school.

00:11:29.030 --> 00:11:31.273
I have not accomplished
anything in my life.

00:11:32.590 --> 00:11:35.803
But that doesn't mean my life is over.

00:11:36.960 --> 00:11:40.513
I don't know very much, I've
not been very many places,

00:11:41.860 --> 00:11:45.360
but I'm about to live a life

00:11:45.360 --> 00:11:50.360
that I could never imagine,
because I'm gonna choose that.

00:11:50.540 --> 00:11:52.320
I'm not a mom.

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:55.523
I'm not a wife or whatever.

00:11:56.610 --> 00:11:58.310
It's just me, myself, and I.

00:11:58.310 --> 00:12:01.270
And I'm gonna make sure that that one day

00:12:01.270 --> 00:12:02.593
when I do become a mom,

00:12:04.060 --> 00:12:06.073
that I'm gonna be the greatest mom ever,

00:12:07.729 --> 00:12:09.227
because I never had that,

00:12:09.227 --> 00:12:12.463
and I'm gonna make sure my
children have that. (sobs)

00:12:14.730 --> 00:12:17.140
It's a harsh reality out here,

00:12:17.140 --> 00:12:19.340
when you see your friends die,

00:12:19.340 --> 00:12:22.033
when you see the people
around you strung out,

00:12:22.870 --> 00:12:25.983
the people you love the most so hurt,

00:12:27.370 --> 00:12:30.410
but all you can do is try
to be a light for them,

00:12:30.410 --> 00:12:33.213
even when you need someone
to be a light for you.

00:12:35.870 --> 00:12:38.120
(sniffles)

00:12:39.784 --> 00:12:42.343
- [Interviewer] Sylvia,
thanks so much for talking.

00:12:42.343 --> 00:12:43.896
Good luck with everything you do in life.

00:12:43.896 --> 00:12:44.896
- Thank you.

